################################################################################
#### OLD COMMIT DESCRIPTIONS ###################################################

FIX Refactorization & clean-up. Music collection

Improved the mutex protection for the screen.
More refactorization and cleaning.
Memory leaks fixed.

A music collection was added to group everything to do with notation
interval calculations, etc. It's kind of a library for basic universal
music theory stuff.











ADD Windows resize detection

The program uses the signal SIGWINCH and ioctl() to keep track
of the terminal window size (following the termcap manual guidelines).

ASCII art for numbers, note names, alterations and the program logo.








ADD terminal capabilities features

User interface experiments just to play with the following functions
(that I need in another project):
	From unistd.h or C standard library"
		isatty
		ttyname
		tcsetattr
		tcgetattr
		ttyslot
		ioctl
	From termcap library:
		tgetent
		tgetflag
		tgetnum
		tgetstr
		tgoto
		tputs

Controlled initialization and termination of the user's interface.
The ECHO capabilities of the terminal is disabled on init.
All three standard terminal-related file descriptors are checked
and the program only launches if all of them are a tty.
The original configuration of the terminal is restored on termination.
The screen is cleared in the beginning and before terminating the
program.

First doxyfile aproximation (the header files headers and
little more).

Re-organization of the header files to avoid forward declarations
for the t_pt type (and probably more to come in the future).










Refactorized, multithread, working note training

The midi interpreter now runs as an independent thread
which made possible to implement the first excercise: play the note.

A random note generator was added.

Now the SIGINT is catched for a controlled exit using an
exit pending flag.

The device file is now opened with O_NONBLOCK to allow a faster
termination of the MIDI listener thread.

No memory leaks detected. A much more cleaner code.




ADD Receiving and interpreting note on/off midi messages

This dirty version is able to locate the device file for MIDI
devices (for ALSA driver) though some hints were included to
support older drivers (e.g. OSS). It can open the device
file and listen to the MIDI messages sent by the keyboard.
So far it supports two types of messages: channel/voice messages:
note on (key is pressed) and note off (key is released).
The program is able to parse these two types of messages and
to decode the key id into a latin note naming style.

This is a draft to proof the concept. The next steps will
probably be to clean it up, re-factor it and probably to advance
into a basic trainer so I can begin to use it to practice my
piano.


